WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:07.040
This ancient religion tells us the truth&nbsp;
about both , Zoro and the sun god nika.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.640
It’s no secret that One Piece is largely&nbsp;
inspired by mythologies from around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.160
world. From Sengoku’s Golden Buddha Mythical&nbsp;
Zoan to the Norse roots of Elbaf’s giants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.840
Oda has clearly studied his myths and&nbsp;
legends extensively. However, One Piece also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.600
shares a lot of its mythology with an ancient&nbsp;
religion that you may not know as much about:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.600
Zoroastrianism. The connection between the two&nbsp;
runs so deep that Oda might have taken inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:43.520
from this religion for at least two of his major&nbsp;
characters. Today we look at the Zoroastrian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:51.280
roots of the Sun God Nika, and even ask: Could&nbsp;
Roronoa Zoro be short for... Roronoa Zoroaster?

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.880
Before I tell you why the answer is probably yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.320
make sure you’re subscribed for more&nbsp;
content like this every single week.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.520
As we wait for Eiichiro Oda to reveal&nbsp;
the secrets of Sun God Nika and Joy Boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:10.960
now is the perfect time to theory craft and see&nbsp;
if we can uncover the rich secrets that make up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.880
the fabric of the One Piece world. While pulling&nbsp;
from so many mythologies from around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:23.120
Oda still manages to weave a cohesive&nbsp;
narrative. That’s an impressive feat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.720
being able to pull together so many&nbsp;
different parts into a solid whole.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:34.160
But perhaps, as user u/jammypants915 **on my&nbsp;
subredit as found out,** there’s something about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.600
these mythologies that helps him along the way.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.880
Oda’s bringing in elements from Abrahamic&nbsp;
religions like Christianity and Islam:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.720
Kuma carries a Bible, and the Quran features a&nbsp;
story of the Zaqqum, a tree in hell that grows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:55.920
actual devil fruits. He also incorporates Eastern&nbsp;
religions like Hinduism and Buddhism - Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.760
we have Sengoku’s Daibutsu fruit and, of&nbsp;
course, Zoro taking the form of Asura.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.920
Oda even borrows from beliefs in Norse or Ancient&nbsp;
Egyptian mythology - we’re all looking forward to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.080
meeting Prince Loki of Elbaf and we remember&nbsp;
Pell and Chaka’s fruits which allowed them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.040
take the shape of animals that resemble Gods&nbsp;
of the Egyptian pantheon. The jackal fruit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.720
essentially turned Chaka into Anubis while the&nbsp;
falcon fruit gave Pell the traits of the Egyptian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:31.040
God Horus. The jury is still out on whether&nbsp;
Horus could also survive massive explosions.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.240
What if these separate influences play together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.560
so nicely because there are common&nbsp;
threads that run through them all? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:47.320
it turns out that almost all of these religions&nbsp;
were influenced or inspired by Zoroastrianism.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:55.040
For anybody who doesn’t know, here’s a brief run&nbsp;
down before we get into the One Piece connections!

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.800
Zoroastrianism is the world’s oldest known&nbsp;
monotheistic religion - or the first major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:07.360
religion to teach that there is only one supreme&nbsp;
God. It comes from Persia located in what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.440
modern day Iran. This faith is based on the&nbsp;
teachings of an ancient prophet called Zoroaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:21.120
or Zarathustra. Some of the defining features of&nbsp;
its belief system center around a duality between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.040
good and evil and the eternal battle between&nbsp;
them. The tenets of Zoroastrianism became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.160
so widespread that they’ve influenced&nbsp;
many other faiths throughout the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.240
including Christianity, Islam, Hinduism, and more.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.600
Zoroastrianism is also one of the world’s&nbsp;
oldest continuously practiced religions with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.040
many people in the world still adhering&nbsp;
to the Zorastrian faith today. One famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.400
practitioner you might be surprised at was&nbsp;
none other than Queen singer Freddie Mercury.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.960
Clearly, the Galley-La Company’s Peeply&nbsp;
Lulu’s resemblance to Freddie Mercury was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:05.600
no mere coincidence and One Piece’s connections to&nbsp;
Zoroastrianism have been hinted at all along…Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.920
maybe that one is a big stretch, but&nbsp;
now that we’ve covered the basics of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.840
Zoroastrianism it’s time to dig into&nbsp;
how it’s inspired One Piece so much!

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.360
Zoroastrian teachings say that the world&nbsp;
was once pure and good, but was slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.480
corrupted by evil forces. However, to&nbsp;
defeat this evil and return the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.800
to its ideal state, every 1,000 years&nbsp;
a hero is reincarnated or reborn until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.880
finally, with the last cycle, the hero&nbsp;
finally vanquishes these forces of evil.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.160
This is a big simplification of some&nbsp;
very complex theology. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.600
you can learn more about it if you get&nbsp;
your hands on a copy of the Avesta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:56.640
the sacred text of Zoroastrianism, but even that&nbsp;
bit of information should scream One Piece to you.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:04.240
One Piece is all about inherited will. Almost all&nbsp;
of the Straw Hats and many other characters have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:10.240
inherited the will of someone close to them. And&nbsp;
Luffy himself seems to share so many parallels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.640
with the former King of the Pirates, Gol D. Roger.&nbsp;
Roger was too early in arriving to Laugh Tale - it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:22.720
seems that there is a cosmic calendar counting&nbsp;
down the time toward some cataclysmic destiny and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:30.080
Luffy is the fated hero to take part in a great&nbsp;
battle to determine the future of the world.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.320
Most recently, we’re seeing that Luffy appears to&nbsp;
be a reincarnation of sorts for Joy Boy. We know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.920
little about Joy Boy so far, but we do know that&nbsp;
he played a significant role in the Void Century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:48.800
which happened nearly 1,000 years ago, somewhat in&nbsp;
line with Zoroastrianism’s cycle of reborn heroes.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.560
Another important connection to&nbsp;
Zoroastrianism comes from Zoro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.560
himself. Is it possible that Oda&nbsp;
got Zoro’s name from this religion?

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:04.880
Most Western viewers, when thinking of the name&nbsp;
Zoro, think of the swashbuckling pulp hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.680
Zorro with two Rs. But despite Antonio&nbsp;
Banderas’s impressive sword wielding skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.440
the rest of the comparisons fall pretty&nbsp;
flat. In fact, we know that Zoro wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.880
actually named after this other hero. And thanks&nbsp;
to the similar name, we have Zorro with two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.680
Rs to thank for every English reader having&nbsp;
to suffer through the official translation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.680
calling our favorite swordsman Zolo.&nbsp;
So, thanks for that, copyright law.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.040
Bonus Fact! If you’re wondering&nbsp;
where the Roronoa part came from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.400
well that’s derived from the&nbsp;
real life pirate Francois&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.320
L’Olonnais. L’olonnais = Roronoa.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.480
Back to Zoroastrianism, though! One of&nbsp;
the most important connections to Zoro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.080
is his Asura technique. Today, Asuras are&nbsp;
known as divine beings within Hinduism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.760
Buddhism. But their lineage can actually&nbsp;
be traced back to Zoroastrianism. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:06.160
the supreme god of Zoroastrianism is known&nbsp;
as Ahura Mazda - The Ahura pronunciation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:12.080
and spelling shifting to Asura as it moved&nbsp;
across different places, times, and languages.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:12.190
This is also where the automobile brand&nbsp;
Mazda got its name. So, if you drive a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:12.320
Mazda then One Piece isn’t the only place where&nbsp;
Zoroastrianism is affecting your life today.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:18.080
Another connection between Zoro and Zoroastrianism&nbsp;
comes through his many brushes with death. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.760
said that in the climactic battle, the great hero&nbsp;
of Zoroastrian myth will cause the dead to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:31.520
reborn and for the souls lost to the underworld to&nbsp;
be purified and brought back to the light. I can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:38.080
help but notice that Roronoa Zoro is currently&nbsp;
having a run-in with the grim reaper itself…but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.800
until we find out more about how that situation&nbsp;
will play out, it’s hard to speculate further.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:49.840
The final connection between Zoroastrianism and&nbsp;
One Piece has to do with fire and light. Luffy has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:55.920
been shown to have unexplained powers to conjure&nbsp;
fire. He is the foretold hero that will bring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:02.240
dawn of the world - the one who will shine light&nbsp;
again on a world that’s been shrouded in darkness.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:09.040
It just so happens that fire and light are crucial&nbsp;
symbols in Zoroastrianism. In this religion there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:15.600
is a practice to light an eternal flame within a&nbsp;
place of worship called a fire temple. When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:22.000
ideas and values of Zoroastrianism are passed from&nbsp;
one person down to the next, it is symbolized by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:28.880
new flame being lit from the existing one. Thus an&nbsp;
eternal flame, one that endures the flow of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:35.200
represents an inherited will not dissimilar to&nbsp;
the concept of inherited will within One Piece.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.880
Speaking of inherited will, in&nbsp;
addition to being a new Joy Boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.680
it would seem that Luffy is also&nbsp;
related to a figure called Sun God Nika.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.240
In Zoroastrianism, fire is used&nbsp;
to represent the god Ahura Mazda.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.240
In addition to this, there is also a&nbsp;
Zoroastrian moral code that follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.600
three main principles. Good Thoughts, or&nbsp;
Pendar Nik, Good Words, or Goftar Nik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:03.360
and Good Actions, or Kerdar Nik. This word&nbsp;
“Nik” which can be translated as “good”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.480
“true”, or “chosen” just so happens to be&nbsp;
where the Persian name “Nika” originates.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:15.920
The name of the sun god Nika literally comes&nbsp;
from Zoroastrianism. If you weren’t convinced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:21.040
any other pieces of evidence, I’m sure&nbsp;
this one will make you think twice.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:27.360
And While we focused on the Zoroastrianism side&nbsp;
of his argument, Oda-sensei also dives deep into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:35.680
Norse Mythology. IT’s the inspiration for both the&nbsp;
giants of Elbaf and our red-haired legend SHanks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.880
Shanks and Luffy are bound to meet very soon.&nbsp;
And if you want to know why it will be on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:47.680
Elbaf and why Luffy must fight his mentor, you&nbsp;
really can’t miss out on this video right here.

